Ah, I've just checked the hearse code and I think I've spotted the 
problem.

The perl script (/usr/bin/hearse) checks the size of the system long
to stop you uploading 64 bit bones files, rather than checking the
actual bones file itself. i.e. it won't correctly detect you running
a 32bit nethack on a 64bit machine.

If you change the following line in the script

my $ulong_size = eval { length pack 'L!', 0 } || 4; # punt for Perl < 5.6

to

my $ulong_size = eval { length pack 'L', 0 } || 4; # punt for Perl < 5.6

It should work.

